/// \defgroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a Update modules and their factory functions (AIGateUpdate to DamageFieldUpdate)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Sixteen of BFME 2's update module names, from AIGateUpdate to
///        DamageFieldUpdate. All sixteen register both factories; this
///        group documents fourteen of the instance factories and eleven
///        of the data factories.
///
/// Each module here is attached to an object by its `Object` block in INI,
/// on a `Behavior = <ModuleName> ModuleTag_...` line
/// (\ref page_modding_modules). This group documents how each module and
/// its data come into existence, the class each module is built on, and
/// the INI fields its data accepts. It does not describe what the modules
/// do every frame. BFME 2 registers many other update modules; only the
/// classes listed below are covered here.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. All sixteen
/// names here are spelled exactly like their classes and are registered
/// with module type 0, the logic (behavior) modules.
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built.
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories walks through this flow
/// step by step; it applies here unchanged. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers, so no direct caller is known. Which class a factory belongs to
/// is established by the registration that pairs it with a module name.
/// The name of the file that currently holds the reconstruction may
/// differ, because files are split, merged and renamed as reverse
/// engineering continues.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from that
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as
///   a C++ new-expression requires. When the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory documented here has the same frame and calls
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc after construction, outside the exception
///   frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new
///   data. The bodies test only the INI pointer before that call, not the
///   new data pointer.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Base classes, read from the retail constructors
/// - Twelve of the fourteen instance classes start their constructor by
///   running UpdateModule's constructor: AIGateUpdate, AISpecialPowerUpdate,
///   AttachUpdate, AttributeModifierAuraUpdate, AttributeModifierPoolUpdate,
///   AutoPickUpUpdate, BannerCarrierUpdate, BloodthirstyUpdate, BoredUpdate,
///   BroadcastStealthUpdate, CivilianSpawnUpdate and CritterEmitterUpdate.
/// - AttributeModifierAuraUpdate and BroadcastStealthUpdate also run
///   UpgradeMux's constructor, and their registrations add the bit Zero
///   Hour uses for the upgrade interface to the update bit, so each is an
///   update module and an upgrade module at once. Zero Hour: UpgradeMux is
///   the base through which upgrade modules respond to upgrades.
/// - ArrowStormUpdate runs the constructor that
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance runs for a plain
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate, and its data class's \c buildFieldParse starts
///   with SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse; it derives from
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate (a structural inference).
/// - DamageFieldUpdate runs FireWeaponUpdate's constructor, and its data
///   class's \c buildFieldParse starts with
///   FireWeaponUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse; it derives from
///   FireWeaponUpdate.
/// - CritterEmitterUpdate's registration adds the bit Zero Hour uses for
///   the collide interface; this page does not confirm what that bit means
///   in BFME 2. The other thirteen registrations carry the update bit
///   alone.
///
/// \par Constructors that draw logic random numbers
/// The constructors of AutoPickUpUpdate, BoredUpdate and
/// CivilianSpawnUpdate each call GetGameLogicRandomValue once
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_random). AutoPickUpUpdate and BoredUpdate
/// draw a value between 0 and the data's \c ScanDelayTime;
/// CivilianSpawnUpdate draws one between half its \c SpawnDelayTime and
/// the whole of it. Each keeps the value in its per-object state. The file
/// names these calls pass for debugging name the original source files
/// AutoPickUpUpdate.cpp, BoredUpdate.cpp and CivilianSpawnUpdate.cpp under
/// `GameLogic/Object/Update`.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has AutoFindHealingUpdate and BoneFXUpdate, with the same INI
/// field names as here. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for
/// ArrowStormUpdate, AttributeModifierAuraUpdate,
/// AttributeModifierPoolUpdate, AutoFindHealingUpdate, AutoPickUpUpdate,
/// BannerCarrierUpdate, BloodthirstyUpdate, BoneFXUpdate,
/// BroadcastStealthUpdate, CivilianSpawnUpdate and CritterEmitterUpdate.
/// AIGateUpdate, AISpecialPowerUpdate, AttachUpdate, BoredUpdate and
/// DamageFieldUpdate appear in neither. All INI field names on these pages
/// are read from the retail executable's own field tables.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and every
///   field on the class pages.
/// - Hard-coded: the sixteen module names, their base classes and what each
///   class does with its fields. A new kind of update needs a new class and
///   a new ModuleFactory::init registration.
/// - AttributeModifierPoolUpdate's module line accepts no fields of its own
///   (see its class page).
/// - Multiplayer: all sixteen run in the game logic, and their data comes
///   from `Object` blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Adding AutoPickUpUpdate, BoredUpdate or CivilianSpawnUpdate to an
///   object, or removing it, changes how many logic random numbers are
///   drawn whenever such an object is built. Every later logic random draw
///   shifts, so replays recorded before the change no longer play back the
///   same way.
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The classes' own duration fields (\c RefreshDelay,
/// \c ScanRate, \c ScanDelayTime, \c SpawnDelayTime, \c ReloadTime and
/// BannerCarrierUpdate's four spawn and respawn times) are parsed by
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into whole logic
/// frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details). A
/// change to the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore needs the
/// INI reloaded, and breaks play with unmodified clients and replays. The
/// random draws above must stay in the same order for lockstep and replay
/// compatibility.
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref sub_rts_model_flow,
///      \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories


/// \class AIGateUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "AIGateUpdate", configured by two
///        trigger widths, along x and along y.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. Its data is AIGateUpdateModuleData. The class is new in
/// BFME 2 as far as the donors show: neither Zero Hour nor Open-BFME-1's
/// \c game/ tree has it. What the module does with its trigger area is not
/// described here.
///
/// \see AIGateUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AIGateUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AIGateUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AIGateUpdate: two trigger widths.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AIGateUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. Its constructor only installs the data's
/// virtual table and sets both widths to zero.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c TriggerWidthX and
/// \c TriggerWidthY, both real numbers parsed by INI::parseReal, default 0.
/// The names suggest the extent of a trigger area along the x and y axes;
/// their unit is whatever the consuming code expects, which is not
/// described here.

/// \fn Module *AIGateUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AIGateUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AIGateUpdateModuleData for this module
///                    line, shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AIGateUpdate, AIGateUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AIGateUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AIGateUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs AIGateUpdateModuleData
/// with both widths at zero, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the
/// block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// AIGateUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, which registers the class's one
/// table and no base table.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AIGateUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, AIGateUpdateModuleData


/// \class AISpecialPowerUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "AISpecialPowerUpdate", whose data
///        names a command button and describes a special power's AI use.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor first, so it is a plain
/// update module. Its data is AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData. Neither Zero
/// Hour nor Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class. The field names
/// suggest it tells computer-controlled play how and where to use a
/// special power; that is an inference from the names, and the module's
/// logic is not described here.
///
/// \see AISpecialPowerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AISpecialPowerUpdate: a command button name, an
///        AI type, a radius, a range and two flags.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c CommandButtonName (text,
/// parsed by INI::parseAsciiString), \c SpecialPowerAIType (a name the
/// parser looks up in a built-in list of 41 names, storing its position),
/// \c SpecialPowerRadius and \c SpecialPowerRange (real numbers), and
/// \c RandomizeTargetLocation and \c SpellMakesAStructure (booleans).
/// - The list of valid \c SpecialPowerAIType names is compiled into the
///   executable; a mod cannot add to it. A name that is not in the list is
///   stored as -1 and no INI error is raised, so a misspelling fails
///   silently.
/// - The button name is stored as text when the INI is read; this page
///   does not establish when, or whether, a misspelled name is reported.

/// \fn Module *AISpecialPowerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AISpecialPowerUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AISpecialPowerUpdate,
///      AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AISpecialPowerUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs
/// AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when \p ini is
/// not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, which registers the
/// class's one table.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AISpecialPowerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AISpecialPowerUpdateModuleData


/// \class ArrowStormUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Special-ability update registered as "ArrowStormUpdate",
///        configured by a weapon, a target radius and shot counts.
///
/// Its constructor runs the constructor SpecialAbilityUpdate's own instance
/// factory runs, so it is built on SpecialAbilityUpdate (see the group
/// page); it then sets up members of its own. The retail executable's
/// debug strings name its original source file ArrowStormUpdate.cpp, and
/// Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class.
///
/// Its registration pairs it with
/// ArrowStormUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, which this group does
/// not document. That data's \c buildFieldParse,
/// ArrowStormUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, registers the shared
/// special-ability fields first (\c SpecialPowerTemplate,
/// \c StartAbilityRange, \c PreparationTime, \c PackTime, \c UnpackTime,
/// \c EffectDuration and others; see
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories) and then its own table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own INI fields, read from the retail table: \c WeaponTemplate (a weapon
/// name, stored as text), \c TargetRadius (real), \c ShotsPerTarget,
/// \c ShotsPerBurst and \c MaxShots (integers) and \c CanShootEmptyGround
/// (boolean). The special power that triggers the ability is a separate
/// \c SpecialPower definition (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades) named
/// by \c SpecialPowerTemplate. How the shots are spread over targets is
/// not described here.
///
/// \see ArrowStormUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *ArrowStormUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ArrowStormUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ArrowStormUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ArrowStormUpdate


/// \class AttachUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "AttachUpdate", configured by a scan
///        filter and range, a parent status, placement options, an FX list
///        and Eva events.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, initialises members of
/// its own and then calls UpdateModule::setWakeFrame with
/// \c UPDATE_SLEEP_NONE, asking to be woken one logic frame later. Its
/// data is AttachUpdateModuleData. Neither Zero Hour nor Open-BFME-1's
/// \c game/ tree has the class. The field names suggest that it attaches
/// its object to a parent found by the scan; that is an inference, and the
/// module's logic is not described here.
///
/// \see AttachUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AttachUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AttachUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AttachUpdate: scan settings, parent status,
///        placement options, an FX list and six Eva events.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AttachUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every object
/// of the template.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table:
/// - \c ObjectFilter (an object filter, parsed by iniParseObjectFilter)
///   and \c ScanRange (real);
/// - \c ParentStatus (object status names, read by the same parser as
///   other status fields such as StealthUpdate's
///   \c HintDetectableConditions and GrantUpgradeCreate's \c ExemptStatus,
///   so it accepts the same names they do);
/// - \c AlwaysTeleport and \c AnchorToTopOfGeometry (booleans);
/// - \c AttachFX (an FX list);
/// - \c ParentOwnerAttachmentEvaEvent, \c ParentAllyAttachmentEvaEvent,
///   \c ParentEnemyAttachmentEvaEvent, \c ParentOwnerDiedEvaEvent,
///   \c ParentAllyDiedEvaEvent and \c ParentEnemyDiedtEvaEvent, all read by
///   one parser.
///
/// The last field name really is spelled with a stray \c t
/// (\c ParentEnemyDiedtEvaEvent) in the retail table; an INI that writes
/// \c ParentEnemyDiedEvaEvent names a field this table does not have.

/// \fn Module *AttachUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AttachUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AttachUpdateModuleData for this module
///                    line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AttachUpdate, AttachUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AttachUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AttachUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs AttachUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and AttachUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse,
/// which registers the class's one table.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AttachUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, AttachUpdateModuleData


/// \class AttributeModifierAuraUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update and upgrade module registered as
///        "AttributeModifierAuraUpdate", configured by a bonus name, a
///        range, a refresh delay and rules for which objects it affects.
///
/// Among other setup, its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor,
/// then UpgradeMux's, and then calls UpdateModule::setWakeFrame with
/// \c UPDATE_SLEEP_NONE, asking to be woken one logic frame later. When
/// the data's \c StartsActive is set, it then calls
/// UpgradeMux::giveSelfUpgrade, so the module is upgraded as soon as it is
/// built instead of waiting for a triggering upgrade (Zero Hour:
/// giveSelfUpgrade plays the upgrade's FX, processes upgrade removals, runs
/// the upgrade's implementation and marks it executed). Its registration
/// carries the update and upgrade bits. Its data is
/// AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has
/// the class; Zero Hour does not. The class and field names suggest an
/// aura that applies a named attribute modifier to objects in range; that
/// is an inference, and how anything is applied or removed is not
/// described here. The separate AttributeModifierPoolUpdate module is
/// documented in this group too, but no link between the two is
/// established here.
///
/// \see AttributeModifierAuraUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AttributeModifierAuraUpdate: the shared upgrade
///        fields plus the aura's bonus, range, refresh delay and target
///        rules.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared
/// by every object of the template. Its \c buildFieldParse adds two tables:
/// first the shared upgrade fields, placed inside this data at an extra
/// offset, then its own table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Shared upgrade fields: \c TriggeredBy, \c ConflictsWith,
///   \c RequiresAllTriggers, \c RequiresAllConflictingTriggers,
///   \c CustomAnimAndDuration and \c Permanent
///   (\ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories describes them).
/// - Own fields, read from the retail table: \c BonusName (text),
///   \c RefreshDelay (a duration), \c Range (real), \c TargetEnemy and
///   \c AllowPowerWhenAttacking (booleans), \c ObjectFilter (an object
///   filter), \c AffectsKindOf, \c StartsActive (boolean),
///   \c RequiredConditions (a set of named flags, parsed by
///   INI::parseBitString32), \c AntiCategory, \c AntiFX (an FX list),
///   \c AffectGood, \c AffectEvil, \c RunWhileDead, \c AllowSelf and
///   \c AffectContainedOnly (booleans) and \c MaxActiveRank (integer).
/// - \c StartsActive = Yes makes the constructor call
///   UpgradeMux::giveSelfUpgrade (see the class page), so the module is
///   upgraded as soon as each object carrying it is built, rather than
///   waiting for an upgrade named by \c TriggeredBy.
/// - \c BonusName is stored as text when the INI is read; the names
///   suggest it selects an attribute modifier definition, but this page
///   does not establish when the name is looked up or whether a misspelled
///   one is reported.

/// \fn Module *AttributeModifierAuraUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AttributeModifierAuraUpdate":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData
///                    for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AttributeModifierAuraUpdate,
///      AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AttributeModifierAuraUpdate":
///        builds the module data for one module line and parses its INI
///        block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs
/// AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse, so the block
/// accepts the shared upgrade fields and the class's own fields.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AttributeModifierAuraUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AttributeModifierAuraUpdateModuleData


/// \class AttributeModifierPoolUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "AttributeModifierPoolUpdate", whose
///        module line takes no INI fields of its own.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, sets up its members
/// (including a vector) and then calls UpdateModule::setWakeFrame with
/// \c UPDATE_SLEEP_FOREVER, so the module starts asleep and does not
/// update until something wakes it. Its registration pairs it with a data
/// factory that BridgeScaffoldBehavior, AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior and
/// ProjectileStreamUpdate are registered with as well. That shared factory
/// builds a minimal module data object and parses the block with a field
/// function that registers no table, so the module line accepts no fields
/// of its own. Open-BFME-1's \c game/
/// tree has the class; Zero Hour does not. What the module keeps in the
/// vector is not described here.
///
/// \see AttributeModifierPoolUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *AttributeModifierPoolUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AttributeModifierPoolUpdate":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The fieldless module data the shared data factory
///                    built for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AttributeModifierPoolUpdate


/// \class AutoFindHealingUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AutoFindHealingUpdate: a scan rate, a scan range
///        and two health thresholds.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AutoFindHealingUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template. The module itself,
/// AutoFindHealingUpdate, is registered with
/// AutoFindHealingUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, which this group does
/// not document.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c ScanRate (a duration),
/// \c ScanRange, \c NeverHeal and \c AlwaysHeal (real numbers). Zero Hour
/// has the same four fields with the same parsers. Zero Hour: for objects
/// of computer-controlled players only, once every \c ScanRate, when
/// health is at most \c NeverHeal times the maximum and the unit is idle,
/// the module sends it to the nearest healing pad within \c ScanRange;
/// the defaults are 0.95 for \c NeverHeal and 0.25 for \c AlwaysHeal, and
/// Zero Hour's \c AlwaysHeal test sits after an early return and never
/// runs. This page does not confirm that behaviour or those defaults for
/// BFME 2.

