/// \class BroadcastStealthUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "BroadcastStealthUpdate", which also
///        reacts to upgrades.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor and then UpgradeMux's
/// and sets up a list member. It then calls UpdateModule::setWakeFrame:
/// with \c UPDATE_SLEEP_FOREVER (asleep until something wakes it) when a
/// text value in its module data is not empty, and with
/// \c UPDATE_SLEEP_NONE (one logic frame later) otherwise. Its
/// registration carries the update and upgrade bits, so the module is both
/// an update and an upgrade. Its registered data factory is not
/// documented in this group. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class;
/// Zero Hour does not. What it broadcasts, and to whom, is not described
/// here.
///
/// \see BroadcastStealthUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *BroadcastStealthUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "BroadcastStealthUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The module data its registered data factory built for
///                    this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see BroadcastStealthUpdate


/// \class CivilianSpawnUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_a
/// \brief Update module registered as "CivilianSpawnUpdate", configured
///        by a spawn delay, a distance, a run-to filter and civilian names.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor and then stores a logic
/// random value between half the data's \c SpawnDelayTime and the whole of
/// it (see the group page). Its registration pairs it with
/// CivilianSpawnUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, which this group
/// does not document; that data's
/// CivilianSpawnUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse registers one table. The
/// retail executable's debug strings name its original source file
/// CivilianSpawnUpdate.cpp, and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has the class;
/// Zero Hour does not. Beyond the timing note below, the module's logic is
/// not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// INI fields, read from the retail table: \c SpawnDelayTime (a duration),
/// \c MaximumDistance (integer), \c RunToFilter (an object filter) and
/// \c Civilian (a list of names, parsed by INI::parseAsciiStringVector and
/// stored as text). The value the constructor draws from the logic random
/// generator is kept per object. The module's update returns it as its
/// sleep time (Zero Hour: the number of logic frames before the update
/// runs again) after each spawn attempt, except on the paths where it
/// returns \c UPDATE_SLEEP_FOREVER instead. The value is therefore the
/// object's interval between spawn attempts, not only a first delay: it
/// varies between objects but is the same on every machine in a game. The
/// constructor does not schedule the first update itself, so this page
/// does not say when the first attempt happens.
///
/// \see CivilianSpawnUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *CivilianSpawnUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CivilianSpawnUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. Its constructor draws
/// one logic random value, so building the module is part of the
/// deterministic game state (group page).
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's CivilianSpawnUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see CivilianSpawnUpdate


